From 2943b4ef45f411487b79742dc95e6ea06a6906de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Fennell <2501251+timfennell@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 10:07:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Insect voids: cross-section void editor, and voids that block
 light

A glossy convex body mirrors its lights as a virtual image behind its
surface, and 3DGS builds that image as splats inside the body, seen through
a half-transparent shell. Two tools keep the body solid and the shine on
its surface (docs/notes/insect-voids.md):

Cross-section void editor ("Keep-out regions (insects)"). Train briefly,
pause, and the current model is cut into cross sections (each splat's
slice through the plane), framed on where the cameras aim so a pin or
mount is left out. Void blobs are drawn on a few key slices and
interpolated between them per blob; they end at their first and last key
unless "Round the ends" is ticked. Saved as roi/voids.svoid and
roi/voids.json (the complement of a stack of outline prisms) and applied
to the paused run or the next one, so splats inside the voids fade.

Voids block light (--roi-void-stop). Keep-out alone leaves the shell free
to stay half transparent: near and far walls together still cover every
pixel, and the shine moves to the inside of the far wall. Now each training
ray ends where it first enters a void, so the near wall must be opaque on
its own. The CPU casts each batch camera against the region (clip to the
voids' bounds from the CSG tree, march, bisect; lens distortion undone),
cached per pose at 512 px; the engine arms a one-shot backend::void_stop
for the training forward only, and the Vulkan 2D raster ends the ray at
the first splat past the void (spec constant kVoidStop). The backward
replays from last_ids unchanged. Vulkan, 3dgs/mip and pinhole cameras;
otherwise the trainer logs why and runs without it. About 2% slower per
step on a 5M-splat run. SS_VOID_STOP_DUMP=<dir> writes the cast maps.

Also: region listing skips macOS "._" AppleDouble files, which broke
loading datasets kept on non-Mac volumes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
